Summary of Case study on UR courses

My group and I discussed UR courses, whether they follow the ACM Code of Ethics, and how helpful they are to students in a case study-like analysis. We accomplished this by going through each General ethical principle to see if they match.

1.1 UR Courses contributes to human well-being and benefits we the students by providing one place where we can access all of our courses and their resources.

1.2 UR Courses helps avoid harm because every user’s account is linked to their activity, which makes it easier to identify and hold accountable anyone who tries to harm another user.

1.3 UR Courses supports honesty and trustworthiness by providing accurate course information, clear instructions, and transparent communication between instructors and students, helping ensure everyone understands expectations and responsibilities.

1.4 UR Courses takes action to promote respectful conduct by leaving a note after logging into the site that says "Student guide: Respectful Conduct in the Learning Environment." It shows that the platform encourages students to follow proper behaviour, sets clear expectations, and reminds everyone to treat each other with respect.

1.5 UR Courses provides a platform where students can access and share course materials, but it is up to the users to respect the work of others, follow copyright rules, and give proper credit for any ideas or resources they use.

1.6 UR Courses respects privacy by allowing users to control their personal information, but features like displaying “online users” by default to all course participants could be improved to give users more privacy and control over their data.

1.7 UR Courses helps honor confidentiality by securely storing student and course information, ensuring that sensitive data is only accessible to authorized users and protected from improper disclosure.

In conclusion, this is as far as our group was able to get in analyzing UR Courses and its alignment with the ACM Code of Ethics before the class continued. It was nice to share ideas and ask questions with the group. Group Discussion- Mtg 10

We had the opportunity to discuss within our group, and we discussed "UR courses supporting the student in education" amongst many of the other topic outlines, we talked about how it might be doing the opposite of support, as many students find it very hard to navigate through the Moodle as well as the wiki in this course, as it can be hard to use, and there might not be a clear direction, and it can appear to be a bunch of links with not so great structure.

A group member mentioned the use of AI which not many members agreed with as it can be used against the student especiallyas there could be leakage of private data which would then lead to a violation ethics. Another member mentioned that there needs to be an investment made in the development of ur courses, an example would be that it could be an app instead of a website, where we can incorporate flashcards, note-taking, and where we can annotate speech-to-text features, which we all agreed was a good suggestion.

feb 5th blog post

Does ur courses support learning??

Not necessarily as urcourses doesn't do anything to support learning and acts more like a log book to store information for your classes. For it to truly support learning alot of features can be added:

* improved digital environment: this can be implemented by using better UI and an improved page

design to promote ease when navigating the course webpage as opposed to having everything listed out and having the user navigate the webpage to find what they’re looking for.

* Being able to access the urcourses as an app instead of the webpage: smaller things like having to login in everytime I want to mark my attendance, check for my lecturers office hours or lecture times. Makes the webpage less and less appealing to use

* Better communication systems between students: A lot of our courses have dedicated Discord groups because it’s way easier to communicate with other students without it feeling like you’re sending a formal email. These Discords usually have different channels for things like assignments, notes and resources, general help, and random questions. That kind of easy, back-and-forth communication directly impacts the learning process, because it makes it easier to ask questions, share ideas, and learn from the same discussion without having to wait a long time for a response from a lecturer or a TA.

Lastly, the implementation of AI-assisted tools: *With the implementation of AI, the learning experience can be better tailored to suit each student’s study style, needs, and questions. One concern that comes up with using AI on a course webpage is academic misconduct. However, with the right guardrails and limitations in place, its use can be narrowed to course-scoped support and prevent it from being used for other purposes.

Adam Raine case

While an argument can be made that, accessibility to information in this case ai is not an issue but rather the reaction from the user to the information given. OpeanAi could’ve done a lot more to prevent the situation, or atleast minimize the damage.

For starters, more effective guardrails should be put in place around chatbots to prevent them from offering any type of “help” or opinion regarding cases like these. For example, Google and its chatbot, Gemini, do a very good job here: if you type anything related to suicide or self-harm into the search bar, it immediately redirects you to a 24/7 suicide hotline for help. On the other hand, according to The Guardian’s publication, chatgpt 4o tried to manage the situation by “helping” adam manage his feelings.

Next, an age restriction on certain topics like with youtube. OpenAI should give parents the ability to supervise and keep sensitive information from their kids while using the chatbot. With this parents can be able to monitor their kids and step in early in the scenario to help prevent their children from doing harmful things.

Lastly, Every chat bot should be required by law to remind it’s users that they are ultimately just large language models and not actual people or specialist to seek advice or help from. A lot of people are in an “artificial intelligence hallucinations”, there are multiple reports of people in relationship and a particular instance of a 14 year old Sewell Setzer III taking his life after months of long emotional and romantic relationship with an AI chatbot in 2024. Even after these reports companies are still profiting off of these type of situations and actively pushing ads towards these chatbots.

UR courses support / Raine Case

I think the UR Courses support system works really well, especially in terms of flexibility. For example, when you’re unable to leave home to speak with someone at the school, you can simply use the UR Courses support tools to get help. This makes access to assistance much easier and more convenient for students.

For the Raine case, I don’t think the chatbot is at fault. When you ask ChatGPT a question, it gives you the information you request and often suggests additional ways to get help. He asked the chatbot for ways and it gave the information requested.

Case Study Like Discussion ~ Lawyer uses fabricated judgments generated

Summary  : In January 2026, Rishi Nathwani KC, a senior lawyer in Australia, took full responsibility for submitting fake quotes and fictitious case judgments generated by artificial intelligence in a murder trial,. The fabrications were discovered by court associates after they could not locate the cited cases, prompting the defense team to admit that they had failed to independently verify every citation after wrongly assuming subsequent AI-generated results would be as accurate as the first ones they checked. Although this blunder caused a 24-hour delay in the proceedings, it did not ultimately affect the outcome for the defendant—a minor—who was found not guilty by reason of mental impairment.

